WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.740
Good Manufacturing Practice requires manufacturing operations to follow approved instructions and

2
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:11.200
appropriate controls. Processes should be defined, suitable for their intended purpose,

3
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:16.780
and performed by trained personnel. Controls must address potential contamination, mix-ups

4
00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:21.820
and errors throughout the process. This introductory lesson explains common operating

5
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:27.260
principles. Specific requirements depend on the product, dosage form and manufacturing

6
00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.010
process.

7
00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:34.210
Incoming starting materials and packaging components should be received, identified, and

8
00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:39.630
controlled according to approved procedures. Appropriate quarantine and release arrangements help

9
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:45.130
prevent unintended use. Storage conditions and stock rotation should reflect material

10
00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:51.990
requirements. Before dispensing, personnel should verify material identity, status and suitability.

11
00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:56.150
A label alone may not be sufficient when supporting records indicate

12
00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.060
a discrepancy.

13
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.120
Dispensing involves selecting and measuring the correct approved materials for a

14
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:08.500
defined batch. Operators should follow authorized instructions, use suitable calibrated instruments,

15
00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:14.000
and document quantities and material identities. Appropriate independent checks or technical

16
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:19.120
controls may be required by site procedures and risk. Traceability connects

17
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.680
each dispensed material to its source and the batch in which

18
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.410
it is used.

19
00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:28.670
Before manufacturing begins, line clearance and setup checks help ensure that

20
00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:33.830
previous materials, labels, documents and residues do not create mix-up or

21
00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:38.870
contamination risks. The equipment, area and materials should be confirmed suitable

22
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:43.630
for the planned operation. Checks must follow approved procedures and be

23
00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:48.790
recorded as required. The exact sequence and verification method depend on

24
00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.150
the facility and process.

25
00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:55.230
In-process controls provide information about the state of a manufacturing process

26
00:01:55.110 --> 00:02:01.470
while it is running. Examples may include weight, volume, temperature, pressure,

27
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:07.250
p H, appearance or other process-specific measurements. Monitoring frequency, limits and

28
00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:11.590
response actions must come from the approved process and supporting scientific

29
00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:17.390
justification. There is no single universal acceptance criterion that applies to

30
00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.930
every pharmaceutical process.

31
00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:25.490
Batch manufacturing records provide traceable evidence of the actual operations performed.

32
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:32.110
Entries should identify relevant materials, equipment, operators, times, process observations and

33
00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:36.950
checks as required by the approved record. Reconciliation can help detect

34
00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:42.590
unexplained differences in quantities or printed materials. Documentation must reflect what

35
00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:48.000
occurred, including deviations, and must not be completed retrospectively from guesswork.

36
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:53.640
Unexpected process results, equipment alarms or procedural departures should be handled

37
00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.780
under the site quality system. Operators should secure the process as

38
00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:03.540
appropriate, report the event and preserve relevant data. Authorized personnel assess

39
00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:09.840
product impact and determine investigation, disposition and corrective actions. A result

40
00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.920
that later returns to normal does not automatically eliminate the need

41
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.710
to document and evaluate the original event.

42
00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:21.370
Imagine an operator preparing to start a batch who discovers that

43
00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.330
a container of starting material has a released label but the

44
00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:30.930
electronic inventory record still shows quarantine status. The operator should not

45
00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.990
choose whichever status seems convenient. The material should be held from

46
00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:41.650
use while authorized personnel reconcile the discrepancy under approved procedures. This

47
00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:48.280
illustrates why status verification, traceability and escalation matter before manufacturing begins.
